+config XTENSA_ISR_NESTING_GUARD
+	bool "Tolerate an unbalanced ISR nesting count"
+	help
+	  The interrupt entry path switches to the interrupt stack only when the
+	  per-CPU nesting count goes from 0 to 1, and the exit path decrements it
+	  without a floor. Anything that unbalances the count -- a context switch
+	  performed from inside an ISR by foreign code, a stray write -- then
+	  makes every later interrupt run on the interrupted thread's stack, and
+	  the machine dies some time later in a way that names nothing. With this
+	  option the entry path treats any count below 1 as "not nested" (switch
+	  stacks, start from 0) and the exit path never goes below 0; both cases
+	  are counted in xtensa_nesting_clamps[] (entry, exit), retained across a
+	  warm reset like the fault breadcrumbs. This does not repair whatever
+	  unbalanced the count; it stops the consequence and reports the event.
+
+config XTENSA_FAULT_BREADCRUMBS
+	bool "Retain fault breadcrumbs across a warm reset"
+	help
+	  Two records that survive a warm reset, for the faults nothing else can
+	  report. (1) At a double exception -- an exception raised while PS.EXCM
+	  was already set, i.e. inside exception or interrupt entry/exit -- the
+	  vector runs no handler at all: it spins until a watchdog resets the
+	  part, and the console shows nothing. With this option the vector first
+	  stores EXCCAUSE, EPC1, DEPC, EXCVADDR, PS, a0, a1, the current thread,
+	  the ISR nesting count and EXCSAVE1 into xtensa_dblexc_record. (2) The
+	  interrupt C handlers count entries, dispatches and undispatched entries
+	  per level and keep the last IRQ dispatched per level in
+	  xtensa_irq_stats, so an interrupt storm that starves the system until
+	  the watchdog names its level and source afterwards. Both live in
+	  .rtc_noinit on Espressif SoCs and in .noinit elsewhere; the application
+	  is expected to copy and clear them early in the next boot (see
+	  <zephyr/arch/xtensa/fault_breadcrumbs.h>). Cost: a few loads and stores
+	  per interrupt.
+
 config XTENSA_SMALL_VECTOR_TABLE_ENTRY
 	bool "Workaround for small vector table entries"
 	help
